═══ R8 EN reply complete (collagen/vegetarian) — guard: passed_after_retry ═══
Q: I have spondylosis and osteoporosis and my doctor requires me to take collagen as part of treatment. But I have been a vegetarian for 14 years. Can I take normal collagen?
Sources: Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) | Buddhism Q&A (EN) | Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) · types: qa_metaphysics,qa_general,introduction
  ✓ substantial reply (>600 chars)
  ✓ ends cleanly (punctuation/emoji, not mid-word)
  ✓ answers the vegetarian-collagen question
  ✓ final text passes guard (0 residual)
--- reply ---
Thank you for asking before taking it — that care for your vow is itself sincerity 🙏

On animal-derived supplements, Master Lu's teaching in *Answered – Frequently Asked Questions About Practising Buddhism* (Q116 section) is quite direct:

> When you consume any medicine or health supplements that contain ingredients such as gelatine or fish oil, if your body does not reject the taste, then perhaps you have not reached the level where you are completely observing your precepts. However, it would still be best to avoid consuming such products as they contain animal by-products.

So ordinary collagen (which is usually bovine, porcine or marine gelatine) is best avoided if you can.

**What I'd suggest practically:**

1. **Ask your doctor for a plant-based / vegan collagen option** or a collagen-builder (vitamin C + amino acids + calcium/vitamin D). Many doctors will accept this once you explain you are a 14-year vegetarian. This solves the problem without touching the precept at all.

2. **If there is genuinely no substitute and it is medically necessary** — treat it as the "no choice" situation Master Lu describes. In that Q&A on eating vegetables from a meat dish, he teaches:

> Under extreme circumstances when you have no choice but to consume those vegetables, you should say to Guan Yin Bodhisattva in your mind that you are consuming these vegetables because you have no choice, and you do not want to eat them at all.

The same intention applies here — tell Guan Yin Bodhisattva clearly, in your own words, that you are taking it as medicine, not out of craving. Master Lu stresses that **intention is what matters most**. If later you feel you have broken the precept, his instruction in that same Q&A is to recite the **Eighty-Eight Buddhas Great Repentance 49 times**, sincerely repent, and then re-make your vegetarian vow.

3. **For the bones, use what Master Lu already recommends for vegetarians:** milk and other dairy products for calcium and other essential nutrients, plenty of soy products (soybeans, tofu, tofu sheets, soy milk) for protein, nuts and multigrain (peanuts, walnuts, chestnuts), mushrooms and fungus, fruits and vegetables for a balanced vitamin and mineral intake — and calcium and soy lecithin supplements if necessary. He reminds us that Buddhas and Bodhisattvas want us to have a healthy body so we can practise better.

4. Keep praying to Guan Yin Bodhisattva for your spine and bones as you do your daily recitation, and continue reciting the **Great Compassion Mantra** for health — Master Lu always teaches medicine and Buddhist practice together, not one instead of the other.

If you'd like someone to look at your case in more detail, the Heart Dharma Door groups have kind volunteers you can speak to:
🔗 https://xlfm.my/contact-us

References: *Answered – Frequently Asked Questions About Practising Buddhism* (Q on consuming vegetables in a non-vegetarian dish; Q on practising vegetarianism); *Metaphysics Q&A Vol. 3* (Q115, Q149)

Namo Greatly Merciful and Greatly Compassionate Guan Yin Bodhisattva 🙏

